{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "נחש שרף ועקרב וצמאון", :translit "nachash saraf va'akrav vetzamaon", :gloss "serpent fiery-serpent and scorpion and thirst"} {:he "בכשדים בפרס במקדון ובשעיר", :translit "bakasdim bapars bamakedon uvase'ir", :gloss "in Chaldeans in Persians in Macedonians and in Seir"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "נחש שרף ועקרב וצמאון", :b "בכשדים בפרס במקדון ובשעיר"}], :frame {:text "The verse (Deuteronomy 8:15) describes the wilderness through which God led Israel as containing snakes, fiery serpents, scorpions, and thirst. Each of these four dangers is read as a code-name for one of four kingdoms that would subjugate Israel across history: the snake corresponds to Babylon (called Chaldeans, already invoked at Genesis 11:28), the fiery serpent to Persia, the scorpion to Greece (called Macedon after Alexander's kingdom, anticipated at Daniel 8:21), and thirst to Rome/Edom (Seir, per Genesis 36:8 where Esau settled in Seir). The identification of Edom with Rome is already implicit in the blessing of Isaac at Genesis 27:39-40, where Esau is told he shall break his brother's yoke.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 8:15" "Genesis 11:28" "Daniel 8:21" "Genesis 36:8" "Genesis 27:39-40"]}}], :arithmetic [{:a-value 1509, :b-value 1508, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "בכשדים בפרס במקדון ובשעיר", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "נחש שרף ועקרב וצמאון"}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:20.462041441Z", :model "glm-5.2"}